# Learning Objectives

@body

- Explain *karyotype preparation*
- Compare *banding* and *chromosome painting*
- Classify chromosome shapes by *centromere position*
- Connect *HeLa cells* with research and consent

=== layout: section

# Karyotypes

=== layout: one-panel

# Chromosomal Abnormalities

@body

- Chromosome changes in humans, other animals, and plants
- Animals generally tolerate these changes less well than plants
- Duplicating an entire chromosome set is not necessarily harmful in plants

=== layout: question-ask

# How Many Sets of Chromosomes in Human Cells?

@notes

Start with somatic cells: two sets of 23 chromosomes, one maternal and one paternal.
Distinguish two chromosome sets from 23 homologous pairs. Human gametes have one set of 23.
Use the following original slide to introduce karyotypes after students respond.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Sets of Chromosomes in Human Cells*

@body

- Human somatic cells have 23 pairs of chromosomes
- A [*karyotype*]{color=red} is an ordered display of the pairs of chromosomes from a cell

