=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_004.png)

Q. You are preparing a karyotype for a patient, during which phase of cell division do you take a snapshot of the cell?

- A. anaphase
- B. interphase
- C. metaphase
- D. prometaphase
- E. telophase

@answer

Answer\: C

=== layout: one-panel

# How Do We Make a Karyotype?

@body

- A *karyotype*: chromosomes displayed in pairs, ordered mainly by size
- Photograph cells when chromosomes are condensed
- Colchicine disrupts spindle formation, arresting dividing cells in metaphase
- Identify chromosomes by:
  - Traditional staining
  - Chromosome painting

=== layout: subsection

# Traditional Chromosome Staining

=== layout: one-panel

# Traditional Staining

@body

- Giemsa staining gives each chromosome a recognizable pattern of [*G-bands*]{color=#00775F}
- [*Dark G-bands*]{color=#00775F}: generally AT-rich; fewer genes
- Reverse staining highlights [*R-bands*]{color=#C80085}: generally GC-rich and relatively gene-rich

=== layout: two-panels

# Heterochromatin and Euchromatin

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_005.png)

@right

- [*Heterochromatin*]{color=#00775F}: more tightly packed in interphase
- [*Euchromatin*]{color=#C80085}: less tightly packed in interphase
- Both condense into visible chromosomes during cell division

=== layout: two-panels

# Different Bands Reveal Different Regions

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_007.png)

- [*G-bands*]{color=#00775F}: relatively gene-poor
- [*R-bands*]{color=#C80085}: relatively gene-rich

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_006.png)

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_008.png)

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

In a human karyotype, chromosomes are arranged in 23 pairs. If we choose one of these pairs, such as pair 14, which of the following do the two chromosomes of the pair have in common?

More than one may be correct.

- A. length
- B. position of the centromere
- C. staining pattern
- D. character coded for by their genes
- E. X-shaped

@answer

Answer\: all of the above

=== layout: two-panels

# Recognizing Homologs: Answer

@left

- [*All of the above*]{color=#007A00}
- length
- position of the centromere
- staining pattern
- character coded for by their genes
- X-shaped

@right

![Original homolog comparison](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_009.png)

@notes

The question concerns chromosomes in a human karyotype: condensed, replicated chromosomes.
The original X-shaped choice refers to that context. Each replicated chromosome has two sister
chromatids; the homologs carry genes for the same characters and may have different alleles.

=== layout: subsection

# Chromosome Painting

=== layout: one-panel

# Chromosome Painting

@body

- *DNA probes*: binding to matching chromosome sequences
- *FISH*: fluorescence in situ hybridization
- Fluorescent labels: visible chromosome sequences
- *Chromosome painting*: probe mixtures; different chromosome colors

=== layout: one-panel

# *Fluorescence in Situ Hybridization (FISH)*

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_010.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Multicolor FISH and SKY

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_011.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# *How Does SKY Work?*

@body

- Large collection of probes. Each probe is complementary to a unique region of one chromosome &#8211; together the "library" is complementary to all of the chromosomes within the human genome.
- Each probe is labeled with a fluorescent color that is designated for a specific chromosome.
  - For example, probes that are complementary to chromosome 1 are labeled with yellow molecules, while those that are complementary to chromosome 2 are labeled with red molecules, and so on.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Comparison*

@body

- There are two slightly different imaging systems that utilize the polarized fluorescence filters to distinguish a mixture of chromosomal paints in a karyotype.
  - multicolor FISH (mFISH; Speicher et al., 1996).
  - spectral karyotyping (SKY; Schrock et al., 1996)
- Main difference\:
  - one image acquisition with post-analysis in SKY versus sequential imaging with specific filters in mFISH.

